<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (J02.0)</content>. Fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior neck nodes and no cough (Centor score 4); rapid streptococcal antigen test positive. No sign of a peritonsillar abscess. The tender, mobile nodes fit the infection, but because lymphoma is still in the differential they will be rechecked once the infection has settled. [S]<list><item>Amoxicillin 500 mg twice daily for ten days; no penicillin allergy.</item><item>Acetaminophen and salt-water gargles for the pain.</item><item>Return for difficulty opening the mouth, drooling, or a voice change.</item><item>Recheck the neck nodes at the six-week visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Primary adrenal insufficiency, stress dosing during a febrile illness (E27.1)</content>. She applied the sick-day rules correctly. With a fever below 39 °C, doubling the hydrocortisone is right; above 39 °C she would triple it. No sign of adrenal crisis: blood pressure steady without a postural drop, no vomiting, no confusion, sodium 136 and potassium 4.7 mmol/L, glucose 91 mg/dL. Fludrocortisone does not need to change. [S]<list><item>Hydrocortisone 20 mg on waking and 10 mg at 2 pm while she has a fever, and for one day after it settles, then back to 10 mg and 5 mg.</item><item>Triple the doses if her temperature goes above 39 °C.</item><item>If she vomits, cannot keep tablets down, or feels faint or confused: inject 100 mg hydrocortisone into the thigh and call 911.</item><item>Salty fluids; continue fludrocortisone 0.1 mg.</item><item>Nurse phone check tomorrow and the day after.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Diagnosis and Treatment of Primary Adrenal Insufficiency: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2016); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis: 2012 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2012)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse phone check tomorrow and the day after. Six-week review as planned, with the neck examined again. Use the injection and call 911 for vomiting, fainting, confusion, or severe abdominal pain. Next scheduled visit 1816-04-30: six-week review: electrolytes, renin, blood count, and recovery. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
